<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>org.os890.jawelte</groupId>
        <artifactId>jawelte-tests-resource-module</artifactId>
        <version>0.3.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
        <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>jawelte-tests-resource-module-scenario-01-lookup-resolves-and-shares-the-instance</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>
    <name>jawelte tests/resource-module scenario 01: @Resource(lookup) resolves, and to the same object @Inject @Named does</name>
    <description>The base claim of the module: a bean declaring @Resource(lookup = ...) against a name a @DataSourceDefinition declared gets a working DataSource, and it is the very same instance the CDI idiom yields. Identity is the assertion that matters — an application mixing both idioms has to be talking to one connection pool, not two.</description>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.apache.xbean</groupId>
            <artifactId>xbean-naming</artifactId>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>
</project>
